  "title": "Advanced TS patterns versus C# source generators",
  "moduleId": "typescript-15-advanced-type-patterns",
  "order": 5,
  "hook": "TS **conditional types** and **brands** are compile-only; C# **source generators** and **records** emit runtime code. Map patterns across stacks.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "What is this?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "TS types erase — pair with Zod at boundaries. C# generators emit IL + code. Brand ≈ record struct wrapper. DeepPartial type-only — use clone at runtime. OpenAPI single schema for both."
        },
        {
          "type": "glossary",
          "title": "Key terms",
          "entries": [
            {
              "term": "source generator",
              "longForm": "Roslyn generator",
              "plainDefinition": "Emit C# at compile.",
              "example": "[Generator]"
            },
            {
              "term": "type erasure",
              "longForm": "TS emit",
              "plainDefinition": "No T in JS.",
              "example": "brands vanish"
            },
            {
              "term": "record struct",
              "longForm": "C# nominal ID",
              "plainDefinition": "Thin wrapper with validation.",
              "example": "record struct UserId(string Value)"
            },
            {
              "term": "OpenAPI",
              "longForm": "shared schema",
              "plainDefinition": "Codegen TS + C#.",
              "example": "openapi-typescript + NSwag"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why does it matter?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "Angular + .NET teams duplicate DTOs. Misapply nominal thinking to structural TS. Interview cross-language mapping."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "variant": "tip",
          "title": "Interview trap: TS types as runtime",
          "body": "strictNullChecks compile-only — validate at boundaries like C# deserialization still throws."
        }
      ]
    },
